{"cuisine/hmong-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/hmong-cuisine","n":"Hmong cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Miao cuisine","cuisine/miao-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in China","intro":[["Hmong cuisine comprises the culinary culture of Hmong people, an Asian diaspora originally from China who are present today in countries across the world. Because Hmong people come from all over the world, their cuisine is a fusion of many flavors and histories in East and Southeast Asia, as well as modern diasporas in the Western world such as the United States. Most dishes are not unique to Hmong culture, but are rather served in a Hmong style developed during centuries of migration across cultures."],["Staple elements include fresh herbs, vegetables such as leafy greens and hot peppers, steamed rice, and small amounts of meat, often chicken meat. Herbs are of major importance as both a food, a flavoring agent, and traditional medicine. Common southeast Asian sauces such as oyster and fish sauce feature prominently, although a fresh sauce called \"pepper dip\" which is unique to Hmong cuisine is so popular that the first ever Hmong cookbook included 11 variations."],["Cooking is traditionally done by steaming or boiling and many dishes are prepared as simple stews or soups. In the modern Hmong diaspora, other forms of cooking such as stir frying have become common. Historically the animal fats and oils required for frying were scarce."],["There is an emphasis on fresh ingredients, as many Hmong are farmers and may pick ingredients from a garden just before cooking. Butchering for meat is also done close to the time of cooking. Frugality is a common theme, as many Hmong are from low-income backgrounds, especially Hmong Americans who immigrated from underfunded refugee camps. Prior to the 21st century, Hmong lived mostly in self-sustaining agricultural villages where they raised livestock and grew crops."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"hmong-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hmong_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"ChawJ Creations","ref":"site/chawjcreations-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"chawjcreations.com","note":"Hmong food and storytelling site by Cindy Her, a pastry chef in the Pacific Northwest.","url":"https://chawjcreations.com/tag/hmong/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Cooking from the Heart: The Hmong Kitchen in America","ref":"cookbook/cooking-from-the-heart-the-hmong-kitchen-in-america","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Sami Scripter and Sheng Yang, 2009","note":"Hmong American home recipes and food traditions, published by the University of Minnesota Press.","url":"https://www.hmongabc.com/collections/books-ntawv/products/cooking-from-the-heart-the-hmong-kitchen-in-america","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":2,"links":[["Diane's Place","dish/diane-s-place"],["Hmong sausage","dish/hmong-sausage"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Ginger","ingredient/ginger"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Thai chili","ingredient/thai-chili"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Miao cuisine","cuisine/miao-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/cuisine-of-east-timor":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-east-timor","n":"East Timor cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Timor-Leste","intro":[["The Cuisine of Timor-Leste consists of regional popular foods such as pork, fish, basil, tamarind, legumes, maize, rice, root vegetables, and tropical fruit. The Cuisine of Timor-Leste is highly influenced by the Malay and Portuguese from historic periods of colonization, immigration etc. Flavors and ingredients from other former Portuguese colonies can be found due to the presence of Portuguese soldiers from other colonies in Timor-Leste."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-east-timor"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_East_Timor"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/pascuense-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/pascuense-cuisine","n":"Pascuense (Easter Island) cuisine","c":"#94bd5c","crumb":[["The Americas","cuisines#the-americas"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Chile","intro":[["Pascuense cuisine, otherwise known as Easter Island cuisine or Rapa Nui cuisine, incorporates the influences of the indigenous Rapa Nui people and Latin America. Notable ingredients include seafood such as fish, octopus (heke), eel, sea snails (pipi) and crustaceans (lobster), as well as sweet potato, taro, banana, pineapple, coconut, pumpkin, and poultry, pork and lamb meat."],["Traditional foods include umu, meat, fish, vegetables and fruit wrapped in banana leaves and roasted in an umu pae, an earth oven. Po'e, pudding made of mashed bananas, pumpkin and flour is baked in the umu pae as well. Other favorite dishes are tunu ahi, fish grilled on hot stones, or ceviche. Pascuense cuisine also includes meat dishes, such as pork or mutton ribs."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"pascuense-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pascuense_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[]}],"cont":"Cuisine of the Americas","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/5/5f/Umu.JPG?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Victorika","lic":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Umu.JPG"}},"ingredient/cornmeal":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/cornmeal","n":"Cornmeal","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 8 dishes across 13 cuisines","intro":[["Cornmeal  is a meal (coarse flour) ground from dried maize. It is a common staple food and is ground to coarse, medium, and fine consistencies, but it is not as fine as wheat flour can be. In Mexico and Louisiana, very finely ground cornmeal is referred to as corn flour. When fine cornmeal is made from maize that has been soaked in an alkaline solution, e.g., limewater (a process known as nixtamalization), it is called masa harina (or masa flour), which is used for making arepas, tamales, and tortillas. Boiled cornmeal is called polenta in Italy and is also a traditional dish and bread substitute in Romania."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-cornmeal"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cornmeal"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":13,"groups":[["American cuisine","cuisine/american-cuisine",[["Grits","dish/grits"],["Hushpuppy","dish/hushpuppy"]]],["Ashkenazi Jewish cuisine","cuisine/ashkenazi-jewish-cuisine",[["Kugel","dish/kugel"]]],["Colombian cuisine","cuisine/colombian-cuisine",[["Arepa","dish/arepa"]]],["Garifuna foodways","cuisine/garifuna-foodways",[["Tamale","dish/tamale"]]],["Israeli cuisine","cuisine/israeli-cuisine",[["Lahoh","dish/lahoh"]]],["Latin American cuisine","cuisine/latin-american-cuisine",[["Tamale","dish/tamale"]]],["Maya cuisine","cuisine/maya-cuisine",[["Tamale","dish/tamale"]]],["Nahua foodways","cuisine/nahua-foodways",[["Tamale","dish/tamale"]]],["Native American cuisine","cuisine/native-american-cuisine",[["Grits","dish/grits"]]],["Paraguayan cuisine","cuisine/paraguayan-cuisine",[["Sopa paraguaya","dish/sopa-paraguaya"]]],["Purépecha foodways","cuisine/purepecha-foodways",[["Tamale","dish/tamale"]]],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine",[["Banosh","dish/banosh"]]],["Venezuelan cuisine","cuisine/venezuelan-cuisine",[["Arepa","dish/arepa"]]]]},{"h":"Where it's grown","grown":{"what":"Maize (corn)","years":"2020 to 2024","url":"https://www.fao.org/fao